  "result": "I fixed the bug: integer ranges with clamping now return values inside the range when a bound is open (`min_open` or `max_open`).\n\n**Cause:** In `IntRange._clamp` (`src/click/types.py:754`), the value was moved past an open bound in the wrong direction. It returned `bound - dir` when it should be `bound + dir`. So with `IntRange(0, 5, min_open=True, clamp=True)`, an input of `-10` was clamped to `-1` instead of `1`. On the upper side, `max_open=True` gave `6` instead of `4`.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `return bound + dir`.\n\n**Checked:** With `IntRange(0, 5, min_open=True, max_open=True, clamp=True)`, inputs `-10, 10, 0, 5` now give `1, 4, 1, 4`. I ran only the range-related tests (27 of them) and they pass; I didn't run the full suite.\n\nRanges with closed bounds were never affected, because they return the bound itself. `FloatRange` doesn't allow clamping with open bounds at all.",
